1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,366
Git worktrees let you check out several branches of one repository at the same time,

2
00:00:05,366 --> 00:00:07,110
each in its own directory.

3
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:13,399
Say you're halfway through a feature, with uncommitted changes,

4
00:00:13,399 --> 00:00:16,478
when a bug report comes in that needs a fix on main.

5
00:00:16,628 --> 00:00:19,108
You could stash your work and switch branches,

6
00:00:19,108 --> 00:00:22,559
but that changes files underneath your editor and running tools,

7
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:24,962
and you have to undo it all afterwards.

8
00:00:25,112 --> 00:00:27,353
Or you could clone the repository again,

9
00:00:27,353 --> 00:00:33,029
which downloads the whole history a second time and leaves you two repositories to keep in sync.

10
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:35,176
Worktrees are a third option.

11
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,746
To see how they work, split a repository into two parts.

12
00:00:40,896 --> 00:00:43,970
The .git directory is the repository itself:

13
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:48,322
every commit, every branch and tag, plus config and hooks.

14
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:50,915
The working tree is the files you edit.

15
00:00:50,915 --> 00:00:54,110
Each checkout also has two pieces of its own state:

16
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:58,983
HEAD, which records what's checked out, and the index, your staging area.

17
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:03,500
A normal clone gives you exactly one working tree for each repository.

18
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,874
git worktree add creates another one.

19
00:01:08,424 --> 00:01:10,997
It creates a new directory called app-hotfix,

20
00:01:10,997 --> 00:01:14,956
starts a branch named hotfix from main, and checks it out there.

21
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:17,550
Your feature directory isn't touched.

22
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:23,427
Both directories share one repository, so history exists once, but each has its own HEAD,

23
00:01:23,427 --> 00:01:24,850
index, and files.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,247
One catch: it's a fresh checkout.

25
00:01:27,247 --> 00:01:31,401
Ignored files like node_modules and .env are not copied over,

26
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:35,514
so install your dependencies again in the new directory.

27
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:41,492
Under the hood, the new directory doesn't get a .git folder.

28
00:01:41,492 --> 00:01:44,160
It gets a .git file, one line long.

29
00:01:44,310 --> 00:01:46,573
That line points into the main repository,

30
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:50,214
where git keeps a small admin folder for each linked worktree.

31
00:01:50,364 --> 00:01:52,773
It holds that worktree's HEAD and index,

32
00:01:52,773 --> 00:01:56,928
plus a commondir file that points back to the shared repository.

33
00:01:57,078 --> 00:02:01,909
Objects, branches, tags, and config all come from that shared store.

34
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:06,090
Because branches live in the shared store,

35
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:10,312
a commit made in one worktree is visible from all the others immediately.

36
00:02:10,462 --> 00:02:12,617
There's no push or fetch involved.

37
00:02:12,617 --> 00:02:17,291
Back in the original directory, git log hotfix already shows the fix.

38
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:20,001
And git branch marks hotfix with a plus sign,

39
00:02:20,001 --> 00:02:22,860
meaning it's checked out in another worktree.

40
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:29,502
There's one rule: a branch can be checked out in only one worktree at a time.

41
00:02:29,652 --> 00:02:34,077
Try to switch to hotfix from the original directory, and git refuses.

42
00:02:34,627 --> 00:02:37,006
If two directories had hotfix checked out,

43
00:02:37,006 --> 00:02:40,064
a commit in one would move the branch under the other,

44
00:02:40,064 --> 00:02:42,969
and its files and index would no longer match.

45
00:02:43,919 --> 00:02:48,599
To look at that code without claiming the branch, add a worktree with --detach.

46
00:02:48,599 --> 00:02:51,979
You get the same commit, with no branch checked out.

47
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:58,081
git worktree list shows every worktree and what it has checked out.

48
00:02:58,231 --> 00:03:03,456
When you're done, git worktree remove deletes the directory and its admin folder.

49
00:03:03,456 --> 00:03:08,143
It refuses if there are uncommitted changes, unless you add --force.

50
00:03:08,293 --> 00:03:11,113
If you delete a worktree folder by hand instead,

51
00:03:11,113 --> 00:03:14,644
git worktree prune cleans up the leftover admin folder.

52
00:03:14,794 --> 00:03:16,513
Neither one deletes the branch.

53
00:03:16,513 --> 00:03:20,417
Your commits stay in the repository until you merge or delete it.

54
00:03:22,333 --> 00:03:25,884
Reach for a worktree whenever you need two branches at once:

55
00:03:26,034 --> 00:03:28,247
a hotfix next to unfinished work,

56
00:03:28,397 --> 00:03:31,520
reviewing a pull request without losing your place,

57
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:35,629
running a slow test suite on one branch while you keep coding on another,

58
00:03:35,779 --> 00:03:40,312
or giving each coding agent its own directory, so their edits never collide.

59
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:46,075
So: one repository, many working directories.

60
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:49,464
History, branches, config, and the stash are shared.

61
00:03:49,464 --> 00:03:53,689
HEAD, the index, and the files on disk belong to each worktree.

62
00:03:53,839 --> 00:03:57,643
And each branch can be checked out in only one place at a time.

